# lts-engine Consolidation [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

> FactLattice's engine capabilities are scattered: QUERY lives in the engine, but ANALYTICS and LINT
> were built in ComposeIO as wrappers around it. This spec consolidates them into ONE coherent PUBLIC
> library — lts-engine (the renamed `factlattice_engine`) — with three clear API layers over the LTS
> graph, retires the MAGMA name, minimizes on-query correctness validation, and splits the public
> library from the private deployment. It is dogfooded: this spec is itself an LTS node the engine can
> query and lint. It turns the "house of cards" into the foundation the bootstrap reads through.

---

## 1. The three API layers

### [LEC-QUERY] Query layer — find / node / traverse
- definition: the existing graph-query surface (`tools.py`: `find`/`node`/`traverse`/`capabilities`/`list_corpora`) — a generic BFS + index lookup over nodes+edges, filtered by the FactLattice-profile predicate surface (domain/function/service/prefix/status/text/since/until/area). It is the FIRST of three co-equal API surfaces, not the whole engine.
- contract: a documented module + MCP tool set + this spec node. Composes over the same graph index + freshness gate as the other two layers.
- lifecycle-position: built
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-ANALYTICS] Analytics layer — deterministic folds over the found set
- definition: the Tier-1/2 aggregation (count/sum/avg/min/max/distinct; group-by + numeric aggregate; 2-D cross-tab; top-k; degree/top-by-degree/count-by-edge-type/orphans) — a deterministic, no-LLM FOLD over `find`'s node set + the freshness-gated adjacency. MOVED IN from `composeio/magma_agg` ([DEC-MAGMA-AGGREGATION-AND-SCALING] realized), where it was a wrapper; inside lts-engine it becomes a native layer.
- invariants: COMPLETENESS (every matching node folded, past the token budget) + the FRESHNESS gate ([INV-MAGMA-FRESH], a stale corpus yields a loud marker never a wrong number) + determinism + honesty (numeric ops skip non-numeric, report skipped).
- lifecycle-position: built
- source: `composeio/magma_agg` @ branch feature/magma-analytics-tier12 (the Tier-1/2 version, NOT main's count/group_by-only)
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-LINT] Lint layer — structural + semantic, unified
- definition: LTS linting as a first-class API surface = the parser's STRUCTURAL checks (dangle / dup-id / ref-integrity, already in `index_builder`) UNIFIED with the SEMANTIC/style layer moved in from `composeio/lts_lint`. One code path so `lints-clean` iff `ingests-clean` ([SPEC:CXC-IC...] the PARSER-LINTER-ALIGNMENT goal made structural instead of conventional).
- contract: a documented module + MCP tool(s) + this spec node; runs over the same corpus the other layers query.
- lifecycle-position: raised
- source: `composeio/lts_lint` + `docs/factlattice-linter/PARSER-LINTER-ALIGNMENT.md`
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-RENDER] Render layer — derived diagrams, never stored ones
- definition: a FOURTH co-equal API surface beside [LEC-QUERY]/[LEC-ANALYTICS]/[LEC-LINT]: render a queried subgraph to a diagram, initially Mermaid. First target is PROCESS FLOWS — `lts_flow(corpus, start_node)` walks a flow subgraph and returns Mermaid text PLUS the structured walk (useful to an agent and to a human). The renderer is subgraph-generic, so supersession chains, dependency trees and decision lineage render with the same code; a flow is simply the first thing pointed at it.
- rationale: David 2026-07-23 — "I'd specifically like for lts-engine to be able to read in a set of nodes describing a process flow and output a mermaid graph in response to the query." His framing is the key insight: A FLOW IS A GRAPH, so this is not a format incompatibility, it is an unused capability plus a missing renderer.
- already-present: the parser ALREADY carries a `temporal` layer with `BEFORE`, plus `CAUSES`/`PRECONDITION_FOR` on the causal layer, and a property->edge table. Nodes, typed edges, layers and a BFS walker all exist. Verified 2026-07-23: there is ZERO mermaid emission anywhere in the engine today.
- gap: (1) BRANCH VOCABULARY — `condition` / `if-true` / `if-false` / `branches` / `converges-to` must become edge-generating properties, which is new corpus vocabulary and therefore belongs with the profile-reconciliation work, NOT free-text; (2) the renderer itself.
- design: walk `part-of`/CONTAINS for membership and the temporal/causal edges for sequence; a node carrying `condition` renders as a decision diamond; `if-true`/`if-false`/`branches` become LABELLED edges; multiple edges into one node yield convergence for free; optional `actor` groups into subgraphs (swimlanes).
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

---

## 2. Engine properties

### [LEC-FRESHNESS] Incremental freshness — minimize on-query validation
- definition: freshness is MAINTAINED, not recomputed per query. Today the engine re-hashes the whole corpus on EVERY call (O(corpus-bytes) I/O tax — the #1 perf finding, [DEC-MAGMA-AGGREGATION-AND-SCALING]). The kernel must maintain freshness incrementally so a query does ~O(0) validation.
- design: content-hash leaves (KEEP the mtime-immune correctness oracle — never trust mtime for correctness), an incrementally-updated root, filesystem-watch for change detection on the long-running MCP stdio server, a one-shot full re-hash on startup/reconnect to seat the root, and a periodic full re-verify as the missed-event backstop. Deployment shape IS long-running (the MCP server persists per session), which is what makes event-driven correct.
- lifecycle-position: built
- as-built: branch `phase-2-freshness` @ 24eb210. Measured 900 -> 0 per-query file-hashes (O(0) holds), ~91x on the query path, and an edit re-hashes 1 leaf of 300 rather than the corpus. Adjacency + degree caches ride the freshness root.
- defect-found-after-build: shipped with a SILENT-STALE WINDOW — see [LEC-SELF-HEAL] `discovered-defect` and [FIX-LTS-PHASE2-SILENT-STALE-WINDOW]. The maintained root only advanced on the watcher poll, so a write between polls was served as fresh over pre-write content. Fixed in phase 3. This node's O(0) claim REMAINS true; what was wrong was the freshness READ, not the hashing economics.
- implication:
    what: "The detailed kernel design + build is a SEPARATE, sequenced follow-on ([LEC-SEQUENCE] phase 2), not part of the structural phase 1."
    why: "It is deep + independent-ish (freshness.py/verdict.py/the store gate); sequencing it after the rename+layers avoids two builds colliding in the same engine package."
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-SELF-HEAL] Stale must be a TRANSIENT, not a terminal state
- definition: on detecting stale, the engine REBUILDS its index and serves the fresh answer, instead of refusing until the process restarts. [LEC-FRESHNESS] bought the CORRECTNESS half (never silently wrong — `success=False, stale=True [INV-LTS-FRESH]`, a loud refusal); it did NOT buy the AVAILABILITY half. The maintained root tells us instantly WHETHER to rebuild; nothing acts on that signal, so the index is never rebuilt and the refusal is permanent.
- why: the engine goes dark exactly when the corpus is being used correctly. Every `decision_add` mutates the decision-graph -> the root moves -> every subsequent `lts_*` query against that corpus refuses until a bounce. Writing a decision BLINDS the writer to the graph it just wrote. This is disqualifying for [LEC-BOOT-READ]/(d): a session-start strategic read that works only while the corpus is inert is not a foundation.
- design: LAZY REBUILD ON DEMAND. The query path, on a stale verdict, rebuilds synchronously (`build_merged_partition` -> `LtsStore` -> `LtsEngine` — the primitive ALREADY EXISTS, proven in `composeio.decision_ops.lts_refresh.LtsRefresher`, which does exactly this for its own in-process view) and then answers. Re-seat the kernel root from the rebuilt index so the next query is O(0) again.
- invariant: the refusal is PRESERVED during the rebuild window — the answer is only served AFTER the index matches the content hash, so the engine still never serves stale. Rebuild failure => still refuse (fail-closed), never serve a partial/old index.
- boundary: watcher-triggered BACKGROUND pre-warm is explicitly OUT of this node — it is a pure latency optimization layered on top later, NOT the correctness path. Keeping the correctness path synchronous and obvious is deliberate in the one component whose whole job is "never lie."
- rationale: David 2026-07-23, on being shown that refusal is already implemented — "basically say, I'm stale, go away? Not sure I want that." Refuse-only is safe and useless; refuse-then-recover is safe and usable.
- cross-process note: this closes the hole flagged in `lts_refresh`'s docstring — a standalone `factlattice_engine.server` cannot see another process's in-process rebuild, so it must be able to heal ITSELF from the content on disk.
- as-built: realized on branch `phase-3-self-heal` @ 9914369 (139 -> 170 tests). Choke point is `LtsEngine._gate()`, so every gated surface heals through ONE path (Analytics inherits it free, being a fold over `find`); `list_corpora` deliberately does NOT heal — it is the inventory/diagnostic tool and must report the raw verdict. The heal is never trusted: `_gate` re-runs the ordinary freshness gate afterward and serves only on a genuinely fresh verdict.
- as-built-refinement: the surgical re-index needed NO splice heuristic, which is the safety argument. `parse_file` is file-local and every cross-file property (node map, id-collision resolution, alias index, per-edge `dangling`, layers, stats) is a pure FOLD over per-file parse results — so only the PARSE is incremental while the whole fold is recomputed on every assemble. An id removed in file A therefore re-dangles edges in untouched file B, and an id added un-dangles them, with no targeted invalidation to get wrong. The [LEC-SELF-HEAL] silent-wrongness risk is closed STRUCTURALLY rather than by special case. Cold build runs through the same path, so healed == cold-start by construction, not by two implementations agreeing.
- discovered-defect: implementing this EXPOSED a real hole in [LEC-FRESHNESS] as shipped — the gate compared against the kernel's MAINTAINED root, which only advanced when the watcher polled (1.0s), so a write landing between polls was answered `success=True` over PRE-WRITE content. Silent stale: the exact invariant [LEC-FRESHNESS] exists to guarantee. Closed by `verdict.PollVerifiedFreshness`, which drives a stat-only hint pass before evaluating — stats only, never content hashes, so the O(0) claim (stated in hashes/bytes) survives. See [FIX-LTS-PHASE2-SILENT-STALE-WINDOW].
- lesson: a test suite that drives the mechanism it is testing cannot find a timing hole in that mechanism — phase-2's tests advanced the root via EXPLICIT poll calls, so the between-polls gap was structurally unreachable by them. Verification must exercise the path a real caller takes, not the path the test controls.
- invalidating_assumption: that a freshness verdict read at query time reflects content as of that instant. If the gate ever reads live content directly rather than a maintained summary, both this node and the watch-lag defect become moot.
- lifecycle-position: built
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST] A missing corpus PATH is a config fault, never an empty-but-fresh corpus
- definition: the freshness kernel's file-is-truth contract ([INV-LTS-FRESH] + [LEC-SELF-HEAL]) is stated for a corpus file that EXISTS and whose CONTENT changed. It says NOTHING about the file being ABSENT. A configured corpus/graph path that does not exist (or is zero bytes) MUST be treated as a HARD CONFIG FAULT, surfaced LOUD, and MUST be distinguishable from a legitimately-empty corpus. It must NEVER be silently reconciled to `live_hash = sha256("")` (`e3b0c44298fc1...`) — because a previously-built non-empty partition then diverges from that empty live hash and the corpus parks in TERMINAL stale (`success=False, stale=True, index_hash=<real>, live_hash=sha256("")`) that NO rebuild can clear: [LEC-SELF-HEAL]'s "rebuild from the file" has no file to rebuild from, so the heal is a no-op and the refusal is permanent, masquerading as ordinary staleness.
- why: 2026-07-30 defect — every `decision_lts_query` returned that exact terminal-stale verdict for corpus `composeux-architect`. Root cause: the corpora reorg (7daf9ef, `R100 decisions/decision-graph-composeux-architect.lts -> corpora/intelligence-console/`) moved the graph, but `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` (in the gitignored `.mcp.json`, so the reorg's tree-rewrite could not sweep it) still named the retired `decisions/` path. The running decision-ops server had built its `composeux-architect` partition while the file still existed there; after the move, live content read as empty -> divergence -> terminal stale, with the misleading engine hint "rebuild the partition" (rebuilding cannot help — the PATH is wrong). A config-drift TRIGGER manifested as silent permanent staleness ONLY because this invariant did not exist to make it loud.
- invariant: decision-ops (and any in-process engine consumer that carries a DEFAULT corpus/graph path from config) MUST validate that path at STARTUP — it exists and is non-empty — and treat a missing/empty default as a HARD CONFIG FAULT surfaced LOUD with a diagnostic naming the path and its env source (`DECISION_OPS_GRAPH`), never silently reconciled to `sha256("")` -> terminal stale. This closes the same class [LEC-CONSUMER-WAIST] closed for engine RESOLUTION, now for corpus PATH existence.
- correction: [2026-07-31, FALSIFIES the "REFUSE TO SERVE / launch-time HALT" enforcement below] — the original enforcement made `main()` print FATAL and EXIT non-zero on the config fault. For a **stdio MCP server** that is self-defeating: the MCP client observes the process die and surfaces only an opaque transport error (**-32000**), and the loud stderr diagnostic is never rendered to the operator — the "loud" fault becomes the SILENT -32000, and a full Claude-Code restart cannot clear it because the config is persistently wrong. The DETECTION (validate at startup, loud, distinguishable from empty) is retained and correct; the RESPONSE mechanism is reconciled by [LEC-DECISION-OPS-BOOTS-AND-REPORTS]: BOOT ANYWAY and report the actionable fault PER TOOL CALL. A dead server cannot tell the operator how to fix it.
- enforced-by: `composeio.decision_ops.mcp_server.validate_default_graph` (raises `CorpusPathError` — the loud DETECTION) + `DecisionOpsMcp.default_graph_fault` (captures it at construction; `dispatch` returns it per graph tool call) + `main()` (logs it to stderr and boots exit-0, never exits on it) + `tests/test_decision_ops_corpus_path_guard.py` and `tests/test_decision_ops_boots_under_bad_corpus.py`.
- boundary: this guards the STARTUP-configured DEFAULT path. Mid-run disappearance of the file under a live server (the exact way THIS instance broke — launched valid, moved out from under it) is a residual the startup guard does not catch on its own; the read path SHOULD also distinguish "corpus file vanished" from "corpus is empty" and surface it loud rather than terminal-stale. Now closed by [LEC-READ-PATH-VANISHED-ROOT] + [LEC-CORPUS-BUILDS-NONEMPTY].
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-CORPUS-BUILDS-NONEMPTY] A configured non-empty corpus that PARSES to zero nodes is a loud fault, never a silent-empty view
- definition: [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST] guards the file's BYTES (exists + non-zero size). It does NOT guard the BUILD RESULT. A default corpus/graph path that exists and is non-empty but whose engine BUILD yields ZERO nodes (`build_merged_partition(...).nodes == []`, i.e. `RefreshResult.ok=True, total_nodes=0`) MUST be a HARD CONFIG FAULT at startup, distinguishable from a legitimately-empty corpus, NEVER a silently-served empty view. This is the failure BAND between "file present" and "file yields content": a path repointed at the WRONG (present) file, a parser/schema regression that no longer extracts this graph's node shape, or an `area=` filter that no longer matches how the graph tags its nodes — all land here, all currently pass [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST] and then answer every query with `total=0` (looks like a fresh, empty corpus).
- why: 2026-07-31 RECURRENCE of the composeux-architect staleness. The 7daf9ef reorg + [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST]/`.mcp.json` repoint made a FRESH launch correct — but the invariant only checked bytes. A configured file that builds to zero nodes (wrong-file drift, parser regression, or `area` mismatch) would sail through the byte guard and serve empty; and a stale cached view whose root VANISHED serves `live_hash=sha256("")` — both indistinguishable from "empty corpus" without this rule. Verified: `LtsRefresher().refresh(<non-empty prose-only .lts>)` returns `ok=True, total_nodes=0` with NO diagnostic.
- invariant: `validate_default_graph` MUST, after the exists+non-empty checks, BUILD the configured graph and REFUSE TO SERVE (loud `CorpusPathError`) if a non-empty source file yields zero nodes — naming the path, `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH`, and the likely cause (wrong-file drift / parser / `area`). Engine-absent (build cannot run) degrades gracefully: file-is-truth, do not block startup on a missing engine.
- enforced-by: `composeio.decision_ops.mcp_server.validate_default_graph` (build-nonempty check) + `tests/test_decision_ops_corpus_builds_nonempty.py` (RED before: a non-empty zero-node file boots silently; GREEN after: startup refuses loud; plus the TRACKED composeux-architect graph builds >0 nodes with a non-empty, fresh live_hash).
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-READ-PATH-VANISHED-ROOT] The read path distinguishes a VANISHED root from an empty corpus, self-heals from the live path, and gives the RIGHT remedy
- definition: when a `decision_lts_query` read returns the terminal-stale marker with `live_hash == sha256("")` (`e3b0c442...`) and a NON-empty `index_hash`, the served in-process partition's ROOT vanished (a moved/renamed corpus, or a cached view built from a since-retired path) — NOT a legitimately-empty corpus. The read path MUST (1) attempt ONE rebuild from the CURRENTLY-configured graph path (self-heal WITHOUT a manual restart — the [SPEC:FL-FRESHNESS] "no manual bounce" bar), so a stale CACHED view over a still-configured file clears itself; and (2) if it is still terminal-stale, surface a decision-ops-level `remedy` that names the ACTUAL fix — repoint `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` at the corpus's current location and relaunch/reconnect the decision-ops MCP — and NOT the engine's misleading `python -m factlattice_engine.build` hint (an on-disk-index rebuild that cannot help an in-process view whose root BINDING is wrong, and is meaningless for a corpus the standalone engine does not even serve).
- why: 2026-07-31 recurrence. The failing read reproduced EXACTLY: `combined_files_hash(["<repo>/decisions"])` (the retired pre-reorg root) `== sha256("")` because that dir holds no `.lts`, while `index_hash` was the old graph content — a partition built while `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` still named `decisions/`, then served after the file moved. The engine's stale marker told the operator to `factlattice_engine.build`, which cannot help; the real remedy was repoint-and-relaunch. This is the read-path residual [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST].boundary flagged and deferred.
- invariant: a terminal-stale-from-empty read is NEVER served as-is with the raw engine hint; the decision-ops read path either self-heals from the live path or returns an accurate, actionable decision-ops remedy.
- enforced-by: `composeio.decision_ops.mcp_server.DecisionOpsMcp._lts_query` (vanished-root detect -> rebuild-from-live -> accurate `remedy`) + `tests/test_decision_ops_corpus_builds_nonempty.py::test_lts_query_vanished_root_self_heals_or_gives_accurate_remedy`.
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-DECISION-OPS-BOOTS-AND-REPORTS] An MCP stdio server ALWAYS boots and reports config faults PER CALL — it never process-exits on one
- definition: a stdio MCP server (decision-ops, and any sibling that speaks JSON-RPC over stdio to a Claude-Code-class client) MUST start successfully (exit 0, stay alive to read stdin) under ANY launch cwd/env, and MUST surface a bad-configuration fault as a clean per-tool-call error result (`{success: False, config_fault: True, error: <actionable diagnostic>}`), NEVER by exiting the process. A `main()` that `return`s non-zero / `sys.exit`s on a config fault is FORBIDDEN for this transport: the client cannot distinguish an intentional refusal from a crash — both collapse to an opaque `-32000` on `/mcp reconnect`, the stderr diagnostic is swallowed, and (because the config is persistently wrong) a full client restart does not clear it. The server that stays alive to SAY "corpus unavailable — repoint `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` and reconnect" is strictly more honest than the one that dies mute. This is [SBR-TRUST] loud-refusal applied to the stdio transport itself: the refusal must reach the operator, so it must be an answered request, not a dead pipe.
- why: 2026-07-31 defect — `decision-ops` returned -32000 on every `/mcp reconnect` AND survived a full Claude-Code quit+relaunch. Root cause (spec-drift): [LEC-CORPUS-PATH-MUST-EXIST]'s enforcement had `main()` exit non-zero on a missing default corpus. The governing config was the HOME-level `~/.mcp.json` (loaded because the session cwd is (a local path, not published), not the repo root), whose `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` still named the retired pre-reorg `decisions/…` path (the 7daf9ef reorg swept the in-repo `.mcp.json` but not the gitignored home copy — a CONFIG fork). Reproduced EXACTLY: launching the server with that env from cwd=home exits 2. Exit-2 -> the MCP client sees the process die -> -32000, opaque and un-actionable. The fail-loud regressed to fail-SILENT precisely because it chose the wrong mechanism for the transport.
- invariant: (1) `main()` NEVER returns non-zero / exits on a corpus/config fault — it captures the fault, logs it to stderr as DEGRADED (not FATAL), and calls `serve()` (exit 0). (2) The op layer carries the fault (`DecisionOpsMcp.default_graph_fault`, computed at construction via `validate_default_graph`) and `dispatch` returns it as a clean per-call error for every graph-consuming tool when no explicit `graph` arg overrides it. (3) Path resolution is cwd-INDEPENDENT: a configured default resolves from its env/absolute value or the module/`COMPOSEIO_ROOT` anchor, never against the process cwd. (`--selftest` and a genuinely unusable interpreter/import may still exit non-zero; a CONFIG fault may not.)
- enforced-by: `composeio.decision_ops.mcp_server.main` (boots exit-0 on `CorpusPathError`) + `DecisionOpsMcp.default_graph_fault` + `DecisionOpsMcp.dispatch` (per-call `config_fault`) + `tests/test_decision_ops_boots_under_bad_corpus.py` (RED before: `main([])` with a missing `DECISION_OPS_GRAPH` returns 2 / the process would die -> -32000; GREEN after: `main` returns 0 and a graph tool call returns the actionable `config_fault` diagnostic).
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-RENAME] Retire MAGMA -> lts-engine
- definition: "MAGMA" (the Haven-origin name) is retired everywhere. MCP server `magma`/`factlattice-magma-lite` -> `lts-engine`; tools `magma_find`/... -> `lts_find`/... (keep the `lts_` prefix); `class MagmaTools` -> `LtsEngine`; every doc/string. ComposeIO side: `magma_agg` moves ([LEC-MOVES]); `magma_perf.py` -> `lts_engine_perf.py`; `docs/magma-primer.md` -> `docs/lts-engine-primer.md`.
- boundary: the ComposeIO MCP registration (transport.sh/.mcp.json `magma` -> `lts-engine`) + the console bounce to re-register are the ARCHITECT's coordinated step — FLAGGED here, not done by the structural build (would break the running consumer mid-flight).
- verify: grep finds NO "MAGMA"/"magma" as a name in the library after the pass.
- lifecycle-position: raised
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-CONSUMER-WAIST] Every in-process engine consumer resolves through ONE shared seat
- definition: any ComposeIO code that reads the lts-engine IN-PROCESS (not over the MCP) MUST resolve + import it through the SINGLE shared engine-path seat — `composeio.decision_ops._enginepath.ensure_engine_on_path` (env `FACTLATTICE_ENGINE_ROOT`, default `~/factlattice-deployment`) — and query it through the engine's PUBLISHED live API (`store.stats`/`store.freshness`/`tools.dispatch`), with the CORPUS SET obtained from the shared corpus seat of [LEC-CORPUS-SEAT] (NOT a bare `config.build_store()`). A consumer MUST NOT fork its own resolution: no hardcoded engine root, no private top-level `factlattice_engine` import path, no retired `MagmaStore`/`MagmaTools`/`magma_*` symbols. Reuse-by-reference, never re-fork ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]).
- why: [LEC-RENAME]/phase-4 (line: "closed three LIVE breakages ... hardcoded dead engine root, `MagmaStore`/`MagmaTools`, `magma_*` dispatch names") fixed those three breakages ONLY in the decision-ops write path, at the SITE. It never stated the GENERAL invariant, so the breakage class was free to recur — and did: the console lts-engine status rail (`intelligence/tools/lts_engine_status.py`, [CXC-DVL-STATUS-SOURCE]), added at 2b099d8 on 2026-07-25 AFTER the re-point, FORKED the pre-consolidation probe — a `~/factlattice` hardcoded root (deleted 2026-07-23) plus a `from factlattice_engine.tools import MagmaTools` (retired symbol). Both raise, so the probe fell through to `unreachable_status()` and the rail showed "engine unreachable" PERMANENTLY while the same engine answered every decision-ops query on the box. A false negative born from a re-fork of the exact pattern the consolidation had already retired.
- invariant: a new in-process consumer that resolves the engine any other way is a DEFECT even if it "works" on the author's box; the shared seat + published API is the only sanctioned path. Enforced by `tests/test_lts_engine_rail_probe.py` (rail reports REACHABLE with real corpus counts through the shared seat; a genuine engine-down still degrades to the honest `unreachable_status()`).
- fixed: `fix/lts-engine-rail-reachable` — the rail's probe de-forked onto `ensure_engine_on_path` + `build_store`/`store.stats`/`store.freshness`, corpus set taken from `store.corpus_ids()` (engine-served truth, not a hardcoded guess). Public contract (`build_status`/`unreachable_status`/bus payload) unchanged.
- lifecycle-position: built
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

### [LEC-CORPUS-SEAT] The in-process seat covers CORPUS RESOLUTION, not just sys.path — a consumer may not depend on the deployment-private corpus config
- definition: an in-process lts-engine consumer MUST obtain its corpus set from the SINGLE shared corpus seat — `composeio.decision_ops._corpusseat.build_engine_store()` — which returns an `LtsStore` serving the DEDUPED UNION of (a) whatever the deployment config serves (`config.build_store()`, when `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG` is present) and (b) the REPO-LOCAL corpora derived from the repo root alone (every `corpora/<id>/` dir plus the standalone `defects/defect-graph.lts`). A bare `config.build_store()` is FORBIDDEN as a consumer's corpus source. Corpus resolution is thus a function of the REPO, and the deployment config is a superset overlay, never a precondition.
- why: `config.build_store()` reads `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG` — a path into the GITIGNORED private deployment overlay ([LEC-SPLIT]). That variable is set ONLY in the `.mcp.json` env blocks `intelligence/launcher/transport.sh` writes, i.e. only for the MCP SUBPROCESSES (lts-engine, decision-ops). The console runtime that hosts the IN-PROCESS sources carries `COMPOSEIO_REPO_ROOT` but NOT `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG` — and neither does a bare `pytest`. With it absent, `default_corpora()` silently falls back to the engine's GENERIC EXAMPLE registry (`alpha`/`beta`/`gamma`), which matches nothing, so `build_store()` returns a store serving ZERO corpora. [LEC-CONSUMER-WAIST] as originally written sanctioned exactly this call, so it steered every new consumer into the trap: the rail's probe then read `store.corpus_ids() == []`, raised "lts-engine reachable but no served corpora matched []", and fail-closed to `unreachable_status()` — the SAME "engine unreachable" false negative [LEC-CONSUMER-WAIST] was written to stop, reached by a different road. The waist had only ever de-forked HALF the seat (the `sys.path` half), so the corpus half stayed forked and diverged three ways: `graph_projection` unions repo-local corpora (correct), `decisions_publisher`/`lts_refresh` build straight from repo files (correct), and the rail took the bare `build_store()` (broken). A fix landed on one copy on 2026-07-25, the sibling copy on 2026-08-05, and the rail was never brought back across.
- invariant: (1) a consumer resolving corpora any other way is a DEFECT even when it works on the author's box — env-dependence on a gitignored private file is exactly the "works here, dark there" failure the waist exists to forbid; (2) the seat's answer must be NON-EMPTY given only a valid repo root, with NO `FACTLATTICE_*` variable set; (3) an engine that is genuinely absent/unimportable still fail-closes honestly (`unreachable_status()`), never a fabricated reachable.
- enforced-by: `python/composeio/decision_ops/_corpusseat.py` (the seat) + `tests/test_lts_engine_rail_probe.py::test_in_process_consumers_resolve_corpora_without_deployment_env` — PARAMETRIZED over every in-process engine consumer, run with `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG`/`FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_ROOT` DELETED from the environment, so a consumer that reintroduces the dependency fails loudly no matter what the invoking shell happens to export.
- lifecycle-position: built
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION]

